@module_doc("""
Hypothetical calls: run a function "as if" and keep only what it computed.

[`speculate`](features/speculate.md) is the full description: what is put
back, which natives run, and how `state` behaves inside the call.
""")

# Package `core`, module `speculate` (core/src/backend/bytecode/vm/speculate.rs,
# docs/features/speculate.md).
#
# Native interface declarations: each `native fn` binds a name to the host
# native of the same name and gives the checker its parameter names, arity
# and types. See docs/usage/ffi.md (Native interfaces).

@doc("""
Calls `f(args...)` against the live program, then puts back every `state`
write, every `set` on a cell older than the call, the random stream, the
noise seed and the print buffer. A native whose effect escapes the run, and
any host-handle method, is not called (it answers `nil`, with a warning once
per call site). Nothing it emitted reaches the host. A `speculate` inside
another is an error.

The result: `value` is `f`'s result; `emits` is what it pushed into output
buffers, as `[{channel, value, at}]` with `at` the `{file, line, column}`
that emitted; and `places` is what `speculate_place` recorded, as
`[{key, value}]`.
""",
  returns: """`{value, emits, places}`.""",
  example: """
    var calls = 0
    fn view(x)
      set calls += 1
      x * 2
    print(speculate(view, 21).value)   # 42
    print(get calls)                   # 0
  """,
  see: ["speculate_value", "speculating", "speculate_place"])
pub native fn speculate(f: function, ...args) -> record

@doc("""
`true` while a speculative call is running, `false` otherwise.
""",
  see: ["speculate"])
pub native fn speculating() -> bool

@doc("""
Inside a speculative call, appends `{key, value}` to its `places`; returns
`value` either way (outside one it only returns `value`). The key must be
one a `for … key` loop accepts (not a function, a cell, a pending value or
NaN).
""",
  returns: "`value`, unchanged.",
  see: ["speculate"])
pub native fn speculate_place(key, value)

@doc("""
`speculate(f, args...).value`, without building the record: the same call,
the same rules, and what it emitted is dropped all the same. For a caller
that wants only the value (`ui_measure`, a derivative probe), it is the
cheaper entry: a call that touches nothing allocates nothing.
""",
  example: """
    fn square(x) x * x end
    print(speculate_value(square, 3))  # 9
  """,
  see: ["speculate"])
pub native fn speculate_value(f: function, ...args)
